{
  "id": "tr-inductors",
  "title": "Inductors",
  "description": "How inductors store magnetic energy and why their current cannot change instantly.",
  "reference": "Hambley §3.4–3.7; H&H §1.16",
  "study": [
    {
      "title": "Inductance and the v–i relation",
      "body": "An inductor is a coil of wire; its current sets up a magnetic flux. A changing current induces a voltage proportional to the **rate of change** of current. With the passive reference configuration (current entering the + terminal): a rising current gives a positive voltage. A constant current gives zero voltage, so in DC an inductor acts as a short circuit.",
      "formula": "v(t) = L\\,\\frac{di(t)}{dt}",
      "circuit": {
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "wire",
            "points": [
              [
                0,
                1
              ],
              [
                1,
                1
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "arrow",
            "from": [
              0.1,
              1
            ],
            "to": [
              0.8,
              1
            ],
            "label": "i"
          },
          {
            "type": "inductor",
            "from": [
              1,
              1
            ],
            "to": [
              3,
              1
            ],
            "label": "L",
            "polarity": "+-"
          },
          {
            "type": "wire",
            "points": [
              [
                3,
                1
              ],
              [
                4,
                1
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "terminal",
            "at": [
              0,
              1
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "terminal",
            "at": [
              4,
              1
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Current from voltage",
      "body": "Integrating the voltage gives the current: the change in current is (area under $v(t)$)/$L$. A constant voltage across an inductor makes the current ramp up linearly.\n**Example:** 5 V across 50 mH for 2 ms changes the current by $5 \\cdot 0.002 / 0.05 = 0.2$ A.",
      "formula": "i(t) = \\frac{1}{L}\\int_{t_0}^{t} v(t)\\,dt + i(t_0)"
    },
    {
      "title": "Stored energy",
      "body": "An inductor stores energy in its magnetic field. The energy depends only on the present current, and it grows with the **square** of the current. Like a capacitor, an ideal inductor stores and returns energy but never dissipates it, so $p = vi$ can be positive or negative.",
      "formula": "w(t) = \\tfrac{1}{2}L\\,i^2(t)"
    },
    {
      "title": "Series and parallel inductances",
      "body": "Inductances combine exactly like resistances: **series inductances add**, and parallel inductances combine with the reciprocal rule (this assumes no magnetic coupling between the coils).",
      "formula": "L_{ser} = L_1 + L_2 + \\dots \\qquad \\frac{1}{L_{par}} = \\frac{1}{L_1} + \\frac{1}{L_2} + \\dots"
    },
    {
      "title": "Current cannot jump",
      "body": "Because $v = L\\,di/dt$, an instantaneous change of inductor current would need an infinite voltage. So the **inductor current is continuous**: $i_L(0^+) = i_L(0^-)$. The inductor voltage can jump.\n**Pitfall:** suddenly opening a switch in series with a current-carrying inductor creates a very large voltage, which causes arcing at the contacts (used on purpose in ignition coils)."
    },
    {
      "title": "Mutual inductance",
      "body": "When coils share magnetic flux, a changing current in one induces a voltage in the other, described by the mutual inductance $M$ (in H). With both currents referenced **into** the dotted terminals (as drawn), the mutual terms are positive. If one current enters a dot and the other leaves a dot, the mutual terms get a minus sign.",
      "formula": "v_1 = L_1\\frac{di_1}{dt} + M\\frac{di_2}{dt} \\qquad v_2 = M\\frac{di_1}{dt} + L_2\\frac{di_2}{dt}",
      "circuit": {
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "terminal",
            "at": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "terminal",
            "at": [
              0,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "terminal",
            "at": [
              4,
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "terminal",
            "at": [
              4,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "wire",
            "points": [
              [
                0,
                0
              ],
              [
                1,
                0
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "arrow",
            "from": [
              0.15,
              0
            ],
            "to": [
              0.85,
              0
            ],
            "label": "i_1"
          },
          {
            "type": "inductor",
            "from": [
              1,
              0
            ],
            "to": [
              1,
              2
            ],
            "label": "L_1",
            "flip": true
          },
          {
            "type": "wire",
            "points": [
              [
                1,
