          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "tr-inductors-1",
      "title": "Voltage, current and energy",
      "kind": "learn",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "i1",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "What an inductor does",
          "body": "An inductor is a coil of wire. Its current produces magnetic flux, and when the current **changes**, the changing flux induces a voltage that opposes the change. The inductance $L$ is measured in henries (H).",
          "formula": "v = L\\frac{di}{dt}"
        },
        {
          "id": "q1",
          "type": "numeric",
          "params": {
            "L": [
              0.01,
              0.05,
              0.1,
              0.2
            ],
            "I": [
              0.5,
              1,
              2
            ],
            "T": [
              0.001,
              0.002,
              0.005,
              0.01
            ]
          },
          "prompt": "The current through a {{L|H}} inductor rises linearly from 0 to {{I|A}} in {{T|s}}. What is the inductor voltage during the ramp (passive reference)?",
          "answer": "L*I/T",
          "unit": "V",
          "explanation": "$di/dt$ = {{I|A}} / {{T|s}} = {{I/T||3}} A/s, so $v = L\\,di/dt$ = {{L|H}} × {{I/T||3}} A/s = {{L*I/T|V}}."
        },
        {
          "id": "q2",
          "type": "trueFalse",
          "prompt": "A constant (DC) current through an ideal inductor produces zero voltage across it.",
          "answer": true,
          "explanation": "True. $v = L\\,di/dt$ and $di/dt = 0$ for a constant current. That is why an inductor acts like a short circuit in DC steady state."
        },
        {
          "id": "i2",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "Energy in the magnetic field",
          "body": "The energy stored in an inductor depends only on its present current. It is released again when the current decreases.",
          "formula": "w = \\tfrac{1}{2}Li^2"
        },
        {
          "id": "q3",
          "type": "numeric",
          "params": {
            "L": [
              0.01,
              0.047,
              0.1,
              0.22
            ],
            "I": {
              "min": 0.5,
              "max": 3,
              "step": 0.5
            }
          },
          "prompt": "How much energy is stored in a {{L|H}} inductor carrying {{I|A}}?",
          "answer": "0.5*L*I^2",
          "unit": "J",
          "explanation": "$w = \\tfrac12 Li^2$ = 0.5 × {{L|H}} × ({{I|A}})² = {{0.5*L*I^2|J}}."
        },
        {
          "id": "i3",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "Inductor current is continuous",
          "body": "A sudden jump in inductor current would need $di/dt \\to \\infty$, so an infinite voltage. In practice the current through an inductor changes **smoothly**: $i_L(0^+) = i_L(0^-)$ whenever a switch operates at $t = 0$."
        },
        {
          "id": "q4",
          "type": "multipleChoice",
          "prompt": "An inductor carries a steady 2 A when the switch in series with it is suddenly opened. What happens?",
          "options": [
            "The current tries to keep flowing, so a very large voltage appears and the switch contacts arc",
            "The current drops to zero instantly with no side effects",
            "The inductor voltage stays at zero",
            "The current doubles"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The inductor current cannot change instantly. Forcing it to zero very quickly means a huge $di/dt$, so $v = L\\,di/dt$ becomes very large, enough to ionize the air across the opening contacts (an arc)."
        },
        {
          "id": "q5",
          "type": "matchPairs",
          "prompt": "Match each quantity with its formula",
          "pairs": [
            [
              "Inductor voltage",
              "$L\\,di/dt$"
            ],
            [
              "Capacitor current",
              "$C\\,dv/dt$"
            ],
            [
              "Inductor energy",
              "$\\frac{1}{2}Li^2$"
            ],
            [
              "Capacitor energy",
              "$\\frac{1}{2}Cv^2$"
            ]
          ],
          "explanation": "Inductors and capacitors are duals: swap $v \\leftrightarrow i$ and $L \\leftrightarrow C$ to go from one formula to the other."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
